WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.668
Environment-driven behavior should be tested directly.

2
00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:09.910
Tests should cover missing variables, malformed values, empty strings, invalid booleans, out-of-range numbers, unsafe paths, conflicting settings,

3
00:00:09.910 --> 00:00:12.578
secret redaction, and production versus development behavior.

4
00:00:12.578 --> 00:00:18.677
A codebase that only tests the default environment can miss failures that appear immediately during deployment.

5
00:00:18.677 --> 00:00:22.488
Logging should help diagnose configuration problems without printing sensitive values.

6
00:00:22.488 --> 00:00:30.874
It is useful to report that a required variable is missing, that a value failed validation, or that a fallback was selected.

7
00:00:30.874 --> 00:00:38.878
It is not safe to dump the full environment or echo secret variables into broad logs, crash reports, or CI artifacts.

8
00:00:38.878 --> 00:00:45.358
CI systems need special attention because they often contain environment variables used for credentials, deployment, or signing.

9
00:00:45.358 --> 00:00:51.837
Build scripts should avoid echoing secret values, and test artifacts should not capture process environments by default.

10
00:00:51.837 --> 00:00:54.505
Secret redaction should be verified, not assumed.

11
00:00:54.505 --> 00:00:57.174
Code review should include environment contract changes.

12
00:00:57.174 --> 00:01:02.891
Adding a new environment variable can alter application security behavior, deployment requirements, and operational procedures.

13
00:01:02.891 --> 00:01:12.039
Reviewers should ask who controls the value, how it is validated, whether it is sensitive, whether it reaches child processes, and how operators will

14
00:01:12.039 --> 00:01:13.944
know what value is active.

15
00:01:13.944 --> 00:01:18.518
Governance does not need to be heavy, but it should be consistent.

16
00:01:18.518 --> 00:01:22.711
Track environment variables in documentation, configuration schemas, deployment manifests, and runbooks.

17
00:01:22.711 --> 00:01:31.859
When a value changes meaning, becomes sensitive, or starts affecting a new process boundary, treat that as a visible change that deserves review and

18
00:01:31.859 --> 00:01:32.240
tests.
